If you live or stay on the west coast most everyday shopping happens in a handful of shopping centres along the coast road. Markets and small village shops sit in between.

## Shopping centres

OpenStreetMap lists three shopping centres on our stretch of coast from north to south:

- **Cascavelle Shopping Village** at Cascavelle inland from [Flic en Flac](/en/areas/flic-en-flac).
- **Le Barachois Shopping Centre** in [Tamarin](/en/areas/tamarin).
- **Ruisseau Créole** at La Preneuse in [Black River](/en/areas/black-river).

Check opening hours with each centre before you go: we do not list hours because they change.

## Markets and village shops

Markets have long been the weekly meeting place where Mauritians buy their vegetables and fruit and meat. Today supermarkets and malls sit alongside them. Further south in [La Gaulette](/en/areas/la-gaulette) and [Le Morne](/en/areas/le-morne) and [Chamarel](/en/areas/chamarel) OpenStreetMap shows no shopping centre. For a big shop drive north.

## What groceries cost

Indicative prices published by the Economic Development Board for Mauritius:

| Item | Indicative price |
| --- | --- |
| Loaf of bread | USD 0.25 |
| Dozen eggs | USD 1.80 |
| Milk (1 litre) | USD 1.25 |
| Water (1 litre) | USD 0.50 |
| Cheese (250 g) | USD 2.20 |
| Potatoes (1 kg) | USD 2 |
| Local beer (330 ml) | USD 1.00 |

## Good to know

- Buying a car in Mauritius comes with 100% excise duty according to the EDB.
- For everything else about living here see [Living in the West](/en/living-in-the-west).
